<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Knox</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1514–<a href="/years/1572">1572</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Knox was the leading figure of the Scottish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. After serving as a galley slave in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and as a minister in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, he worked with Calvin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, and on his return in 1559 his preaching helped spark the Protestant revolt. He shaped the Reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a> established in 1560, opposed Mary, Queen of Scots, and wrote a History of the Reformation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-knox.jpg" alt="John Knox" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Theodore Beza, Icones (1580) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knoxbezaicones.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Reformation led by <b>John Knox</b> produced a Calvinist church that shaped Scottish society, education and ideas of resistance, and the National Covenant of 1638 helped to start the Wars of the Three Kingdoms that convulsed Britain and Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From Geneva and other centres the Reformed faith spread to France, the Netherlands, parts of Germany, Hungary and Scotland, where <b>John Knox</b> led the break with Rome that the Scottish Reformation Parliament made law in 1560.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · People</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Haddington, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.96,-2.78&amp;year=1551" aria-label="Haddington on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4489 258 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Knox"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4939 528h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4939 528h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.96,-2.78&amp;year=1551">What was here in 1551? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Conscience, Madam, requires knowledge; and I fear that right knowledge ye have none.&#8221;</p><footer>History of the Reformation in Scotland, book 4 (modernized edition, 1905) &#183; original &#183; his own account of his interview with Mary, Queen of Scots, 1561 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48250/pg48250-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Madam, as right religion took neither original strength nor authority from worldly princes, but from the Eternal God alone, subjects are not bound to frame their religion according to the appetites of their princes.&#8221;</p><footer>History of the Reformation in Scotland, book 4 (modernized edition, 1905) &#183; original &#183; his own account of his interview with Mary, Queen of Scots, 1561 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48250/pg48250-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;God forbid, that ever I take upon me to command any to obey me, or yet to set subjects at liberty to do what pleaseth them. But my travail is that both princes and subjects obey God.&#8221;</p><footer>History of the Reformation in Scotland, book 4 (modernized edition, 1905) &#183; original &#183; his own account of his interview with Mary, Queen of Scots, 1561 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48250/pg48250-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If their princes exceed their bounds, Madam, no doubt they should be resisted, even by power.&#8221;</p><footer>History of the Reformation in Scotland, book 4 (modernized edition, 1905) &#183; original &#183; his own account of his interview with Mary, Queen of Scots, 1561 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48250/pg48250-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Now, Madam, if I had intended to have troubled your estate because ye are a woman, I might have chosen a time more convenient for that purpose than I can do now, when your own presence is within the realm.&#8221;</p><footer>History of the Reformation in Scotland, book 4 (modernized edition, 1905) &#183; original &#183; his own account of his interview with Mary, Queen of Scots, 1561 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48250/pg48250-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
